exports[`the workflow files a broken workflow: a warning each, and the job stays green 1`] = `
"## Sluiceway check

The setup is valid.

No sluiceway.yaml, so every setting is its default.

### Stacks

Found no stacks.

### Files that no stack claims

Every file is claimed by a stack, covered by scan.unrelated, or one of the docs and tooling files that force nothing by default.

### Workflows

| Workflow | Job | Mode | Action ref |
|---|---|---|---|
| .github/workflows/deploy-dashboard.yml | sluiceway | auto | at main, which is not a release |

- .github/workflows/deploy-dashboard.yml, job sluiceway: sluiceway/sluiceway@main is not a release. A branch runs code that is not released yet. Use a major tag such as @v0 to follow every release, an exact tag such as @v0.8.0, or a full commit SHA.
- .github/workflows/deploy-dashboard.yml runs on pull&#95;request, and its Sluiceway job loads the credentials of your stacks before it. On pull&#95;request those steps would run code that is not on the default branch yet. Keep the check in a workflow of its own, which needs no credentials.
- .github/workflows/deploy-dashboard.yml has no workflow&#95;dispatch trigger. The rescan box and settle start a scan through it.
- .github/workflows/deploy-dashboard.yml, job sluiceway: the modes it runs need actions: write. A job's own permissions replace the workflow's.

The check reads the workflow files as text. The repo's default token permissions, the rules of an environment and what GitHub itself validates live elsewhere and do not show here.

### What a check cannot tell

A check reads files only, so it cannot say that a preview will work: a stack that does not exist in the backend, a missing credential or a registry the runner cannot reach shows only in a scan.

With backend: true the check also asks the backend which stacks it holds, with the credentials of its job.
"
`;
